.TH SOLAR_SYSTEM 6 "2026-10-30" "2.0" "Simulation"
.SH NAME
solar_system \- interactive 3\-D N\-body simulation of the solar system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B solar_system
.br
.B solar_system \-\-test
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B solar_system
integrates the Sun and the eight planets as a full N\-body gravitational
system with a 4th\-order symplectic integrator and renders them in an
interactive 3\-D view with orbit ellipses, fading trails, an arcball camera,
a follow\-the\-body focus mode, an optional swarm of asteroid and Kuiper
belt test particles, and a live data panel showing the calendar date, the
drift of the conserved quantities, and Mercury's measured perihelion rate.
.PP
Initial conditions are the JPL Solar System Dynamics Keplerian elements
for the J2000.0 epoch (2000\-01\-01 12:00 TT).
All bodies exert gravity on all others.
An optional first post\-Newtonian (general\-relativistic) correction
reproduces Mercury's 43\(dq/century perihelion advance.
.PP
The same source builds a native binary and a WebAssembly version for the
browser; see
.BR BUILDING .
With
.B \-\-test
the physics self\-tests run without opening a window; see
.BR TESTS .
.SH CONTROLS
.SS View
.TP
.B Drag
Rotate the view (arcball).
.TP
.B Shift + drag
Pan (turns follow mode off).
.TP
.B Scroll wheel
Zoom in / out.
.TP
.B Click a body
Select it.  Clicking a name in the panel does the same.
.TP
.B 1\-9
Select a body (1 = Sun, 2 = Mercury, \&... 9 = Neptune).
.TP
.B F
Follow mode: centre the selected body and zoom so its whole orbit fits;
the camera then tracks it.
Selecting another body re\-fits.
.TP
.B Z\~/\~X\~/\~Y
Top\-down view of the ecliptic (XY), edge\-on (YZ), edge\-on (XZ).
.TP
.B R
Reset view, zoom and pan; leaves follow mode.
.TP
.B H
Show / hide the key\-binding overlay.
.SS Simulation
.TP
.B Space
Pause / resume.
.TP
.B T
Reverse the direction of time.
The integrator is time\-symmetric, so the system retraces its path.
.TP
.B +\~/\~=
Faster: more integration steps per rendered frame (up to 6000, i.e.
500 simulated days per frame).
.TP
.B \-
Slower (down to 1 step = 2 hours per frame).
.TP
.B G
Toggle the 1PN general\-relativistic Sun\(enplanet term.
Resets the perihelion\-rate statistics.
.TP
.B I
Toggle the integrator between 4th\-order Yoshida (default) and 2nd\-order
leapfrog, to compare their numerical perihelion drift and energy error.
.TP
.B A
Toggle 1500 main\-belt and 500 Kuiper\-belt massless test particles.
.TP
.B V
Start / stop MP4 video capture (native build only; requires
.BR ffmpeg (1)
in PATH).
Output is written to the current directory as
.IR solar_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.mp4 .
.TP
.B Q\~/\~Esc
Quit.
.SH DATA PANEL
.SS Header
Calendar date (TT) and elapsed time; speed in simulated days or years per
real second, derived from the measured frame rate; the relative drift of
total energy and angular momentum since t = 0 (with GR on, the Newtonian
energy is not conserved, and the line says so); the integrator and step;
and Mercury's secular perihelion rate ϖ̇,
a least\-squares slope of the osculating longitude of perihelion with its
standard error and the time span used.
Expect about +530\(dq/century from planetary perturbations alone and
about +573\(dq/century with GR on.
.SS Selected body
Semi\-major axis, eccentricity, inclination and period from the catalogue
elements; current heliocentric distance, instantaneous speed next to the
catalogue mean speed, and the current osculating eccentricity from the
integrated state; radius and mass.
.SH PHYSICS
.SS Integrator
Yoshida's 4th\-order symplectic composition: three kick\-drift\-kick
leapfrog sub\-steps of lengths
.IR w\d1\u\~dt ,
.IR w\d0\u\~dt ,
.I w\d1\u\~dt
with
.I w\d1\u = 1/(2\-2\u1/3\d)
and
.IR w\d0\u = 1\-2w\d1\u ,
time step 2 hours.
Being symplectic it conserves a modified Hamiltonian, so energy oscillates
about a constant instead of drifting; being 4th order its numerical
perihelion drift for Mercury is ~1.5\(dq/century, versus ~22,000\(dq/century
for plain leapfrog at the same step (press
.B I
to see this live).
Velocities are kept synchronised with positions (velocity Verlet), so no
half\-step offset is applied at initialisation.
.SS Relativity
With
.B G
on, each planet receives the dominant first post\-Newtonian acceleration
in the Sun's field,
.IR a " = \(*m/(c\(S2r\(S3) [ (4\(*m/r \- v\(S2) r + 4 (r\(mdv) v ]",
with
.IR \(*m\~=\~G(M☉+m) .
This produces Mercury's extra 42.98\(dq/century.
Planet\(enplanet 1PN terms are omitted.
.SS Units
Length in AU, mass in solar masses, time in years, so that
.I G = 4π²
and
.IR c\~=\~63241.077\~AU/yr .
.SS Initial conditions
Preferably exact JPL Horizons barycentric state vectors at J2000.0
.RI ( horizons_j2000.h ,
generated by
.IR tests/fetch_j2000_state.sh ;
the Makefile uses it when present).
Otherwise from the JPL SSD table of approximate mean Keplerian elements
(J2000.0), which are not osculating and give percent\-level position errors
for Saturn within decades:
semi\-major axis, eccentricity and inclination are used directly;
the argument of perihelion is
.I ω = ϖ − Ω
and the mean anomaly is
.IR M₀ " = L − ϖ" .
Kepler's equation is solved by Newton iteration and the in\-plane velocity
uses the exact expression
.IR v " = n·a² / r"
with
.IR n\~=\~\(sr(\(*m/a\(S3) .
.SS Test particles
Asteroids are massless bodies integrated by leapfrog with a 1\-day step
under the nine massive bodies (cost O(N\(muM)); they feel the planets but
do not perturb them.
Elements are drawn deterministically: main belt 2.1\(en3.3 AU, Kuiper belt
39\(en48 AU.
.SH TESTS
.B solar_system \-\-test
(also
.BR "make test" )
checks, without a window:
vis\-viva at t = 0 for every planet;
Earth's two\-body period against Kepler's third law;
energy and angular\-momentum conservation over 100 years of the full
N\-body problem;
Mercury's numerical perihelion drift and its 1PN advance (42.98\(dq/century);
and, if
.I tests/horizons.csv
exists, the planets' positions against JPL Horizons.
.I tests/fetch_horizons.sh
downloads that file for a chosen epoch.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOLAR_FONT
Path to a TrueType font to use instead of the bundled / system fonts.
.TP
.B SOLAR_SCALE
Native only.
Initial window scale factor (e.g. 1.5 opens 2400\(mu1350).
The window is resizable and HiDPI\-aware in any case.
.TP
.B SOLAR_SNAPSHOT
Native only.
Path of a BMP file: render some frames, save the frame and exit.
.B SOLAR_SNAPSHOT_FRAMES
(default 30) sets how many;
.B SOLAR_SNAPSHOT_KEYS
is a string of keys to press first, e.g.
.B az
for the top view with belts.
Combine with
.B SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy
for a fully headless render.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/solar_system/DejaVuSans.ttf
Font installed by
.BR "make install" ;
searched first, so the installed binary needs no system fonts.
.TP
.I web/index.html
WebAssembly build: one self\-contained file (code, Wasm and font embedded).
.TP
.I docs/index.html
Copy of the web build for GitHub Pages when deploying from a branch.
.SH BUILDING
.TP
.B make
Native binary (requires SDL2 and SDL2_ttf development headers).
.TP
.B make test
Run the physics self\-tests.
.TP
.B make install
Install binary, font and this man page under
.I ~/.local
(override with
.BR PREFIX=... ).
.TP
.B make wasm
WebAssembly build (requires Emscripten; source
.I ~/emsdk/emsdk_env.sh
first).
.TP
.B make pages
Copy the WebAssembly build to
.I docs/
for GitHub Pages deployed from a branch.
The repository also ships a GitHub Actions workflow that runs the tests
and deploys Pages on every push to main.
.TP
.B make zip
Package the WebAssembly build for itch.io.
